# The terminal's insert mode (IRM), on lines of every size. CSI 4 h sets
# insert mode and CSI 4 l resets it (ECMA-48, IRM). In insert mode, each
# printed character pushes the text from the cursor to the line's right
# edge one place right, and the character pushed past the edge is lost, as
# on the VT510. Nothing moves to the next row. On a line of double size the
# right edge is the line's last column, half the screen's width in columns,
# since each character there takes two columns.
#
# The typing pad echoes each key as a line editor's sequence and nothing
# more, so it shows the terminal's raw VT510 behaviour. Two steps, whose
# titles start with "Explore", show raw behaviour that looks broken on its
# own: a letter typed at the right edge, and one line of a tall pair edited
# alone. Those steps have no right or wrong answer, quote the manual, and
# ask the maintainer to press s. Tier 1 checks those rules: the cases
# edit-irm-wrap, line-size-irm-wide and line-size-irm-tall in
# tools/conformance/vtium. The other steps are checks. The pair-mode step
# checks what a program written for the VT510 shows: it sends each edit to
# both lines of the tall pair, so the tall letters stay whole.
#
# Each step runs the typing pad, tools/vt/checks/typepad.py, with --insert,
# so F2 turns insert mode on and off and the key list shows whether insert
# mode is on. The insert screen holds a line of normal size, a wide line,
# an empty line and a tall pair, all but the empty line full of letters.
# make vt-check runs the guide from the checkout's top directory, so the
# steps name the pad by that relative path.
#
#   make vt-check GUIDE=insert

title: the terminal's insert mode on lines of every size

intro: Each step starts the typing pad. The pad shows what the terminal does
intro: when a program only echoes the keys you type. The terminal never moves
intro: text to another row when you insert a letter. Text flows onto the next
intro: row only when a program redraws the line itself, as zsh and vim do.
intro: Steps whose titles start with "Explore" have no right or wrong answer.
intro: They show the terminal working as DEC's manuals describe. Press s
intro: when you are done with one, and add a note if you like. A separate
intro: proof editor, which a later card builds, is to show a VT510-capable
intro: editor that authors mixed character sizes, placement and wrapping.
intro: The pad shows the step's text at the top of the screen, then its keys,
intro: then the lines to type on. F2 turns the terminal's insert mode on and
intro: off, and the key list says whether insert mode is on. Ctrl-D ends the
intro: typing. A wide line has letters twice as wide as normal. A tall pair
intro: is two lines that show the top and bottom halves of the same letters.

step: Insert mode pushes letters right on a normal line
run: python3 tools/vt/checks/typepad.py --insert insert
do: The cursor starts on the k of the first line. Press F2, so that the key
do: list says "Insert mode: on". Type a few letters.
expect: Each letter goes in at the cursor and pushes the letters after it one
expect: place right. The letter at the right edge disappears each time.
expect: Wrong: letters that replace the letters under the cursor.

step: Explore a letter typed at the right edge in insert mode
run: python3 tools/vt/checks/typepad.py --insert insert
do: Press F2, then End. Type one letter, then one more. Try more keys if
do: you like.
expect: There is no right or wrong here. The first letter takes the right
expect: edge, and the letter that stood there is lost. The next letter starts
expect: the wide line below. The terminal works as the VT510 manual says of
expect: insert mode: "Characters moved past the page's right border are
expect: lost". Tier 1 checks this rule. Press s when you are done.

step: Insert mode on a wide line
run: python3 tools/vt/checks/typepad.py --insert insert
do: Press Down, onto the wide line. Press F2 and type a few letters.
expect: The new letters are wide and whole. The wide letters after the
expect: cursor move one place right, and stay wide and whole. The wide letter
expect: at the right edge of the screen disappears each time.
expect: Wrong: a narrow letter, half of a letter, or a letter lost in the
expect: middle of the screen.

step: Explore insert mode on one line of a tall pair
run: python3 tools/vt/checks/typepad.py --insert insert
do: Press Down three times, onto the upper tall line. Press F2 and type a
do: few letters. Try the lower line too if you like.
expect: There is no right or wrong here. Only the line the cursor is on
expect: changes, so the tall letters after the cursor show split halves. The
expect: terminal works as the VT100 User Guide says: "the same character
expect: output must be sent to both lines to form full double-height
expect: characters". Tier 1 checks this rule. Press s when you are done.

step: Pair mode keeps tall letters whole in insert mode
run: python3 tools/vt/checks/typepad.py --pair --insert insert
do: Press Down three times, onto the upper tall line. Press F2 and type a
do: few letters. Then press Backspace, Insert and Delete twice each.
expect: The pad sends each edit to both lines of the pair, as a program
expect: written for the VT510 does. After each key, every tall letter is
expect: whole, the new letters too.
expect: Wrong: a tall letter whose two halves do not match.

step: With insert mode off, letters replace
run: python3 tools/vt/checks/typepad.py --insert insert
do: Press F2, then F2 again, so that the key list says "Insert mode: off".
do: Type a few letters.
expect: Each letter replaces the letter under the cursor. The letters after
expect: the cursor stay where they are.
expect: Wrong: letters pushed right.

step: Typing on a wide line wraps at the right edge
run: python3 tools/vt/checks/typepad.py --insert insert
do: Leave insert mode off. Press Down, onto the wide line, then End. Type
do: three letters.
expect: The first letter replaces the wide letter at the right edge of the
expect: screen. The next two letters start the empty line below, at normal
expect: size.
expect: Wrong: a letter past the right edge, or a letter that wraps in the
expect: middle of the screen.
